{"type":"text","timestamp":1790854316591,"sessionID":"ses_f08c46ba3ffeXIM05O83TMKJzd","part":{"id":"prt_0f73c1e53001NrI6RXNSj7vzCe","messageID":"msg_0f73bf49d001jg0IW3DKsBFfv7","sessionID":"ses_f08c46ba3ffeXIM05O83TMKJzd","type":"text","text":"{\"action\":\"finish\",\"answer\":\"Fixed the overlap detection for half-open integer intervals in intervals.py.\\n\\nBug: `overlaps` used `max(left[0], right[0]) <= min(left[1], right[1])`. The non-strict `<=` wrongly reported two cases as overlapping:\\n- Touching intervals, e.g. [0,1) vs [1,2) — they share no integer point since end is exclusive.\\n- Any interval vs an empty interval sharing a boundary, e.g. [2,2) vs [1,3) — an empty interval contains no points.\\n\\nFix (intervals.py, `overlaps`): changed `<=` to strict `<`, so the condition is `max(starts) < min(ends)`. This matches the docstring semantics \\\"share an integer point\\\": the intersection [max(starts), min(ends)) is nonempty only when its start is strictly below its end.\\n\\nBehavior check (manual case analysis; no runtime execution was available in this environment, so this is reasoned verification, not a test run):\\n- Disjoint [0,1) vs [2,3): 2 < 1 → False (preserved).\\n- Overlapping [0,2) vs [1,3): 1 < 2 → True (preserved).\\n- Touching [0,1) vs [1,2) and reversed order: 1 < 1 → False (fixed).\\n- Empty [2,2) vs [1,3), [2,5), or [2,2): → False (fixed); symmetry holds since max/min are commutative.\\n\\nPublic API preserved: same function names and signatures (`overlaps(left, right)`, `span(interval)`); `span` untouched.\\n\\nSource reference: intervals.py, function `overlaps` (file sha256 before edit: 95d7e0377e63f77ddfd8916f81a19f98647e64471c48ca760a8732b01b8bd679; after: 9e70bef1c695f947a9682d7d2ced85d7fa3caf14435060bbe49270044c27207d).\"}","time":{"start":1790854307411,"end":1790854316579}}}
